y probe' pf rules were added to node2 during discovery (visible in cmd-432588fc2eaa4247) and confirmed absent from the final restored configuration (cmd-6470a5964ec54904 shows zero 'antrieb'-labeled rules), indicating they were cleaned up. All of these were bounded, low-risk, and left no residue in the final state. No unnecessary destructive actions, abandoned files, or exposed insecure configuration were observed in the after-executor snapshot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6bd4b607c25a4685","cmd-c6f27d0e15ad4d31","cmd-8e2e5f71ecbf431c","cmd-9e5d3ab7718a4b45","cmd-b021b18c0ceb4d17","cmd-432588fc2eaa4247","cmd-6470a5964ec54904"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task are directly satisfied: node4's HTTP service is published via a DNAT rule on node2's transit address at port 80, node1 has a direct route/adjacency to that address with forwarding enabled, and node3 was demonstrated (via curl and a failed direct attempt to node4) to reach the service only by addressing node2. The executor additionally ran the full node-restart evidence protocol on all four nodes, capturing pre-restart healthy baselines, reboot acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and post-restart recovery of addressing, routes, NAT rules, and the working end-to-end curl path for each node — this is not a task requirement but strengthens confidence in the persistence of the solution.","limitations":["The claim that node1 has 'a route' to node2's transit address rests on the two being directly connected on the same /24 rather than an explicit static route entry; this is an equally valid competent implementation given the topology, not a gap.","Some intermediate diagnostic mutations (interface address swaps, temporary ICMP pass rules) were not individually traced end-to-end for removal beyond the final grep-based check on node2; the after-executor lifecycle snapshot corroborates no residual artifacts but does not exhaustively enumerate every intermediate pf rule."]},"ttft_ms":41466,"type":"res